      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sure thing—here's my sanity check on what you've pasted:\n\n**Potential issues to flag before merging:**\n\n1. **Stateless migration risk:** Moving from persistent sessions to stateless Lambda triggers means any agent logic relying on in-flight conversation context will need externalized state (DynamoDB, Redis, etc.). If that's not in the PR, downstream consumers will silently lose context.\n\n2. **Orphaned dependencies:** Deleting `orchestrator_service` likely strands any module importing it—worth grepping the codebase for references to `agent_core.runtime` and `orchestrator_service` before merge, since Python won't complain until runtime.\n\n3. **Missing deprecation window:** Sunsetting autonomous capabilities entirely with no grace period or feature flag is risky for production consumers. A phased rollback or at least a stub returning a deprecation warning would soften the break.\n\n4. **Event contract defined?** \"Event-driven functions\" implies a defined event schema. If Lambda triggers expect a payload shape that isn't pinned anywhere, you'll get integration drift between producers and consumers.\n\nIf the PR includes only the deletion plan without these mitigations, I'd hold off on LGM until state management and the event schema are addressed.",
